  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nThe current regional MRMS mosaic shows the system taking shape \nto our southwest. It will continue to move eastward overnight \nand the initial warm air advection push as it nears may bring \nsome light rain to southern New Hampshire. Otherwise, the main \nsystem pushes into southern New Hampshire after sunrise and will\ninitially be all rain (except for maybe in the highest \nelevations of the Monadnocks and White Mountains). Rain reaches \ninto the southern Maine coast in the afternoon as the initial \nlow pushes off the coast and out to sea. Again, high \ntemperatures climb into the 40s area wide, so snow will only be\npresent at high elevations through today.\n\nThe story changes a bit as we see the secondary low develop and\nfollow in the wake of the first one. Temperatures are expected \nto bottom out near or below freezing by Friday morning which \nmeans tonight is the best chance for snow flakes to make it the \nsurface. However, accumulation is still a big question and there\nare a few factors that are going to affect it. The first is \nmarginal temperatures; while everyone has a chance to see flakes\ntonight they may not last long after hitting the \"warm\" ground,\nor accumulations may only occur on elevated or grassy surfaces.\nThe second factor is dry air being pulled in from the north as \nboth lows move east of the area. This is going to dictate the \nnorthern extent of the precipitation shield and the hi-res \nmodels are beginning to start resolving that. I certainly think \nthe 12.00Z HRRR is overdoing it, but I could see a situation \nwhere northern New Hampshire and the Western Maine Mountains see\nlittle or nothing. I have moderate confidence in precipitation \nat least making it up into the foothills. Here is the breakdown:\n\nQPF and Snow - Guidance continues to struggle with the exact track \nand speed of the low, and a closer pass and longer residence time \nwould increase totals. However, the consensus seems to be on the \nfurther east track with the low being progressive; the result is \nlight QPF and thusly snow totals. The REFS mean shows about a half \ninch to an inch of QPF in southern New Hampshire and far southern \nMaine, with only a quarter to a tenth up into the New Hampshire and \nMaine foothills. Most of this is going to fall as rain before the \nchange over tonight, which is why I think outside of the mountains a \ndusting to a half inch of snow accumulations are all this system is \ngoing to be able to muster, especially after factoring in\nsurface temperatures. The caveat here is that the WPC snowband \nprobabilities are non-zero in southern New Hampshire and should\na band develop, elevated rates would lead to better \naccumulation, but with the marginal surface temperatures my gut \nsays even this scenario likely only amounts to an inch or two.\nElevations over 1000 feet stand the best chance of seeing \nseveral inches.\n\nWinds and Coastal Flooding - Both a non-issue in this case. The \nfurther east track only brings 20-25 mph winds to the coastal plain \nwith lighter winds inland. Tides are not particularly high either \nright now, so even with a foot or two of surge we remain \ncomfortably below flood thresholds.\n\nEarly morning commuters will want to be wary of slick spots Friday, \nbut overall the system will be exiting and precipitation tapers off \nthrough the morning. The upper low hangs around through the weekend \nso showers continue as well, mainly in the mountains, but nothing \nof significance. High pressure than builds in for at least the \nfirst half of next week bringing drier weather and trending \ntemperatures back to seasonable normals.",
